@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:apps.subcomponent.data_table-header_cell a ds:Subcomponent;
    ds:name "DataTable.HeaderCell";
    ds:summary "The header cell is the interactive column header element within a data table. It handles sorting, resizing, reordering, and pinning for its column. It is a sub-component and cannot function outside of a data table.\n\n\n\nThe header cell is distinct from the body cell. While the body cell displays data values and provides row-level interactions such as inline editing and row actions, the header cell is responsible for column-level controls that affect how the entire column behaves.";
    ds:anatomyDsl "";
    ds:anatomyClassic "https://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3340-5297&t=8RN2Hpk8UX3gMX3u-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=4604-105392&t=2fO23I8WsyT0YVJ1-4";
    ds:usage "Every column in the data table has a header cell. Its primary purpose is to display the column label. The label should clearly and concisely describe the data shown in the column, so users can scan the header row and immediately understand what each column contains. Choose labels that are specific and unambiguous. For example, \"Last updated\" is more useful than \"Date\", and \"Assigned to\" is clearer than \"User\". Beyond labeling, the header cell also serves as the interface for column-level controls such as sorting, resizing, reordering, and pinning. The designer configures which of these features are available on each column. Not every column needs all capabilities. Focus on enabling the controls that match how users work with the data in that column.\n\n### Sortable columns\n\nSortability is not enabled by default. The designer must explicitly enable sorting on each column. Enable sorting only for columns where the sort order is obvious and helpful to the user. For example, sorting on a MAC address column would not be obvious or helpful, so it should not be enabled. Similarly, utility columns such as an actions column (containing edit/delete buttons) should never be sortable.\n\n### Column resizing configuration\n\nResize handles are shown by default, but the designer can choose to hide them.\n\nColumns that have been resized by the user count as fixed-size columns and no longer participate in the automatic growth behavior of the columns, unless the size is reset to auto (through double-selecting the resize handle or pressing Option + 0).\n\nThe fixed width of a resized column is persisted in browser storage across sessions.";
    ds:guidelines "# Interactions\n\n## Sorting\n\n### Sort interaction cycle\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-53162&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-54052&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-54942&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nSorting follows a three-step cycle on the column header:\n\n1. **First select - sort ascending.** The sort icon changes from the neutral “sort both” (up-and-down arrows) icon to the “sort up” icon. The header cell receives focus (visible focus ring).\n2. **Second select - sort descending.** The icon changes from “sort up” to “sort down”. The arrow animates with a 180-degree clockwise rotation from up to down.\n3. **Third select - return to unsorted.** The icon returns to the neutral “sort both” state. The table data returns to its original unsorted order.\n\nAfter the cycle completes, the next interaction starts the cycle again from ascending.\n\n### Header cell states\n\n**Default (unsorted, not hovered)**\n\nNo sort icon is shown on sortable column headers in their resting state.\n\n**Hover**\n\n**https://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-53162&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4**\n\nWhen the user hovers a sortable column header, the header cell receives a hover background color and the cursor becomes a pointer. The “sort both” icon appears (if the column is not currently sorted) to indicate the column is sortable and interactive.\n\nIf the column is already sorted (ascending or descending), hovering still shows the pointer cursor, and the existing sort direction icon remains visible. The header stays interactive.\n\n**Active sort column (not focused)**\n\n**https://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-55830&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4**\n\nWhile a column is the active sort column, its header cell retains the hover-style background color persistently, even when the cell is no longer focused. The sort direction icon (up or down) remains visible. This persistent highlight communicates which column the table is currently sorted by.\n\n**Focused**\n\n**https://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-56714&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4**\n\nImmediately after selecting to sort, the header cell shows a blue focus ring in addition to the hover-style background and the sort direction icon.\n\n### Sorting with pagination\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-57610&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\n**Offset pagination**\n\nIf the table is offset-paginated and the user is not on page 1, selecting a sort header triggers a modal warning before sorting is applied. The warning informs the user that sorting will reset pagination back to page 1. After the user confirms, the table is sorted and pagination resets to page 1.\n\n**Sorting with selection (simple selection)**\n\nIf the table has an active simple selection (not persistent selection), the modal also warns that the selection will be dropped. If both pagination reset and selection drop apply, the two messages are combined into a single warning.\n\n**Warning modal**\n\nThe modal has the title “Sorting will reset pagination and selection” and the body text: “Are you sure you want to sort? Sorting the table differently will reset the pagination and selection.” It contains a “Don’t ask again” checkbox, a “Cancel” button, and a primary action button labeled “Sort and reset the table”. The user can dismiss this warning permanently through the checkbox.\n\n**Keyset pagination**\n\nSorting is not practical on keyset-paginated tables. Sorting must be disabled entirely on keyset-paginated tables. If sorting is needed for a given use case, change the table to offset pagination instead.\n\n\n\n### Backend vs. frontend pagination\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-58594&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nWhen the table is paginated on the backend, selecting a sort column puts the table body into a loading/skeleton state while the newly sorted page is fetched. Column headers remain visible and readable during loading. Only the body rows show gray skeleton placeholder bars. Once the data is fetched, the table displays the sorted results.\n\nIf the table is paginated on the frontend, the sorted results can be shown immediately without a loading state.\n\n### Double row state\n\nIn a double row configuration (where columns are collapsed into stacked rows within cells due to a narrower viewport), a column can only be sorted by its first row.\n\nIf the second row was previously an independent column that was sorted, and the user then decreases the viewport so that column becomes the second row of a double row, the table remains sorted by that second-row value. The header it belongs to shows the sorting state. However, selecting that header cell sorts by the first row instead. After that point, sorting by the second row is no longer possible while in the double row state.\n\n## Column resizing\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-60460&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-61348&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-62238&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-63126&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\n### Resize handles\n\nResize handles are thin vertical lines shown at the right edge of each column header. They indicate that a column can be resized. The interaction target for the resize handle is more generous than it appears, to make it easier to grab even for users with lower dexterity. The selectable area extends beyond the visible thin line.\n\nResize handles do not appear:\n\n- **After a checkbox column.** The checkbox column cannot be resized. No resize handle appears between the checkbox column and the first data column.\n- **After the last column.** There is no resize handle at the trailing edge of the last column.\n\n### Mouse interaction\n\n**Header cell hover**\n\nIf a cell header is hovered, the background color change does not extend to the resize handle. The hover background intentionally stops short of the resize handle area.\n\n**Resize handle hover**\n\nWhen the user hovers over the resize handle, the cursor changes to `col-resize`. After hovering the resize handle for 500 ms, the column line gets highlighted with a more visible vertical line extending from the header row down through all data rows. This provides a clear visual indicator of which column border will be resized.\n\n**Dragging**\n\nOnce the user starts dragging the resize handle, a blue “focus” line appears at the column’s edge to indicate which column border is being resized. The column resizes by the amount the user drags left or right. A column cannot be resized beyond its min-width. Only horizontal mouse position changes are taken into account. Vertical movement is ignored.\n\n**Double-select to reset**\n\nThe user can reset a column’s custom size back to the default growth behavior by double-selecting the resize handle.\n\n### Keyboard interaction\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-64014&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-64911&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-65810&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-66694&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\n**Initiating a resize**\n\nTo resize a column with the keyboard, the user must first focus the header cell (the header cell shows a visible focus ring).\n\nThe user then presses **Option/Alt + Left/Right arrow keys** to resize the column. While this combination is held, the resize column border indicator (the same one used for mouse interaction) is shown instead of the header cell focus ring.\n\n**Debounce behavior**\n\nThere is a slight debounce after the user stops pressing the keyboard combination, so that the UI does not jump between focus on the header cell and focus on the column border if the user pauses briefly (for example, stops for half a second) and then resumes resizing. After the debounce completes, focus returns to the header cell.\n\n**Reset with keyboard**\n\nIf the user presses **Option/Alt + 0** while a column header is focused, the column size resets to the default auto-sizing behavior.\n\n\n\n## Column reordering\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-67592&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-68484&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-69377&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-70279&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-71181&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-72085&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-72987&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\n### Mouse / pointer interaction\n\n**Hover state**\n\nIf the user hovers a header cell, it enters the hovered state and shows the sort icon. This is the entry point for initiating a drag.\n\n**Starting a drag**\n\nIf the user starts dragging, the hover effect on the column header disappears (including the sort both-ways icon). Instead the whole column receives a **3px focused outline**.\n\nAbove the dragged column, a **gray-translucent ghost column** appears. It follows the user’s pointer.\n\n- The **focused column** indicates what column is being dragged.\n- The **ghost column** indicates where the column is being dragged to.\n\n**Moving the ghost column**\n\nAs the user moves while dragging, the ghost column follows. A **drop zone indicator line** also appears. It indicates where the column would be dropped if the user released the column.\n\n**Drop position logic**\n\nWhere the column will be dropped is determined by the edges of the ghost column crossing the **horizontal midpoint** of a target column. For example, if the right edge of the ghost column has crossed a target column’s midpoint, the dragged column will be dropped to the right of that target column.\n\n**Releasing the column**\n\nWhen the user releases the ghost column, the actual column moves to where the drop zone indicator line indicated. **The move is animated.**\n\n**Completed state**\n\nAfter the column has been moved:\n\n- The drop zone indicator line disappears.\n- The column is no longer focused.\n- The ghost column disappears.\n\nThe table returns to a clean, resting state with the new column order.\n\n\n\n### Keyboard interaction\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-73870&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\nhttps://www.figma.com/design/WGHcOmGMEUF9hMAEoRafL8/%F0%9F%93%95-Pragma---documentation-visuals?node-id=3486-74768&t=2PBanDq6QA2yNcI2-4\n\n**Initiating a keyboard move**\n\nTo move a column with the keyboard, the user must have a table header cell focused.\n\n**Key combination**\n\nIf a table header cell is focused and **Shift + Arrow Left/Right** is pressed, the column moves left or right (if it can).\n\n**Animation and focus retention**\n\nThe move is animated. The header cell **remains focused** after the move, allowing continued keyboard interaction without re-focusing.\n\n### Constraints\n\n**Immovable columns**\n\n- **Checkbox column:** The checkbox column cannot be moved, and no column can be moved before the checkbox column. It serves as the fixed left boundary.\n- **Action column:** The action column cannot be moved, and no column can be moved after the action column. It serves as the fixed right boundary.\n\n**Pinned column behavior during drag**\n\n- If a **pinned column** is dragged between non-pinned columns, that column becomes **non-pinned**.\n- If a **non-pinned column** is dragged to the left of a pinned column, it becomes **pinned** as well.\n\n## Column pinning\n\n### How to pin a column\n\nThe user pins a column through the **table settings panel** (opened from the gear icon on the far right of the header row). The settings panel displays a list of all columns, each with a pin icon and a delete icon. Selecting the pin icon on a column pins it.\n\nWhen the user **hovers** the pin icon, a tooltip appears explaining what the button does. The tooltip also states that pinning only takes effect when the table is horizontally scrolling, so users are not confused if pinning has no visible result on a table that fits within the viewport.\n\n### Column repositioning on pin\n\nIf the user pins a column that is not already adjacent to the left edge, the column is **moved to the left** into the pinned column group. The settings panel column order updates to reflect the new arrangement.";
    ds:tier ds:apps;
    ds:documentationStage ds:tag.needsdefinition;
    ds:hasProperty [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "label";
        ds:summary "The column name displayed in the header cell. Should clearly and concisely describe the data in the column.";
        ds:propertyType "text";
        ds:optional "false"
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "sortable";
        ds:summary "Whether the column header responds to sort interactions. Enable only for columns where the sort order is obvious and helpful to the user.";
        ds:propertyType "boolean";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "false";
        ds:constraints "Not available on checkbox or action variants. Disabled entirely on keyset-paginated tables."
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "resizable";
        ds:summary "Whether the resize handle is shown at the right edge of the header cell.";
        ds:propertyType "boolean";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "true";
        ds:constraints "Not available on checkbox or action variants."
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "reorderable";
        ds:summary "Whether the column can be dragged to a new position through the header cell or the settings menu.";
        ds:propertyType "boolean";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "true";
        ds:constraints "Checkbox and action columns are never reorderable."
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "pinnable";
        ds:summary "Whether the column can be pinned to the left side of the table through the settings menu.";
        ds:propertyType "boolean";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "true";
        ds:constraints "Subject to smart pinning constraints. Pinning only takes effect during horizontal scrolling."
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "sort-direction";
        ds:summary "The current sort state of the column. Follows a three-step cycle: ascending, descending, unsorted.";
        ds:propertyType "choice";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "unsorted";
        ds:constraints "Only applies when sortable is true."
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "column-width";
        ds:summary "The width of the column. Defined as a fractional unit (fr) for proportional sizing or a fixed pixel value. Columns resized by the user become fixed-width and no longer participate in automatic growth.";
        ds:propertyType "text";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:defaultValue "auto"
    ], [
        a ds:Property;
        ds:name "min-width";
        ds:summary "The minimum width of the column. A column cannot be resized below this value.";
        ds:propertyType "number";
        ds:optional "true";
        ds:constraints "Must be a positive value in rem."
    ];
    ds:parentComponent ds:apps.pattern.datatable.
